The Stan programs in tests/testthat/test-bcm-*.R are not original to frmtmb.
Each one is adapted from the corresponding model in the Bayesian_Cognitive_
Modeling directory of:

  stan-dev/example-models. https://github.com/stan-dev/example-models

  Stan translation of these models was done by Martin Smira; see
  https://github.com/stan-dev/example-models/tree/master/Bayesian_Cognitive_Modeling

That repository ships no LICENSE file. Its README states the terms:

  "All of the example models are copyrighted by their author(s) or assignees
  under the new BSD license unless another open-source license is explicitly
  stipulated in the directory containing the model."

The Bayesian_Cognitive_Modeling directory stipulates no other license, so the
new (three-clause) BSD License applies, reproduced below. BSD-3 is compatible
with GPL (>= 2) and the combination is distributed under the GPL, as the first
line of this file says.

Every test file that carries an adapted program names the upstream directory,
the license, and the adaptations made, at the head of the file; the two
adaptations applied throughout are recorded in tests/testthat/helper-stan.R.
No program is copied verbatim: every sampling statement is rewritten as
`target += ..._lpdf(...)` so that Stan drops no normalizing constant, and the
generated-quantities blocks are dropped. No JAGS, WinBUGS or hBayesDM source
was read or copied; the models themselves are the book's published equations.

The models and the data are from:

  Lee MD, Wagenmakers EJ (2013). Bayesian Cognitive Modeling: A Practical
  Course. Cambridge University Press.

The data sets transcribed into the test files are the book's own, and are
originally from:

  Riefer DM, Knapp BR, Batchelder WH, Bamber D, Manifold V (2002). Cognitive
  psychometrics: Assessing storage and retrieval deficits in special
  populations with multinomial processing tree models. Psychological
  Assessment, 14, 184-201.  (the MPT category counts)

  Heit E, Rotello CM (2005). Are there two kinds of reasoning? Proceedings of
  the 27th Annual Conference of the Cognitive Science Society.  (the
  hierarchical signal detection counts)

  Kruschke JK (1993). Human category learning: Implications for backpropagation
  models. Connection Science, 5, 3-36.  (the generalized context model data)

  Murdock BB (1962). The serial position effect of free recall. Journal of
  Experimental Psychology, 64, 482-488.  (the SIMPLE serial position data)

  Rubin DC, Hinton S, Wenzel A (1999). The precise time course of retention.
  Journal of Experimental Psychology: Learning, Memory, and Cognition, 25,
  1161-1176.  (the memory retention counts)

  Geurts HM, Verte S, Oosterlaan J, Roeyers H, Sergeant JA (2004). How specific
  are executive functioning deficits in attention deficit hyperactivity
  disorder and autism? Journal of Child Psychology and Psychiatry, 45,
  836-854.  (the model selection rate data)

  Zeelenberg R, Wagenmakers EJ, Shiffrin RM (2004). Nonword repetition priming
  in lexical decision reverses as a function of study task and speed stress.
  Journal of Experimental Psychology: Learning, Memory, and Cognition, 30,
  270-277.  (the within-subject rate data)

  Bem DJ (2011). Feeling the future. Journal of Personality and Social
  Psychology, 100, 407-425.  (the extrasensory perception data)

The psychophysical function data are the book's case study data, credited
there to Kuijpers and colleagues; the balloon task data are the book's
"George" records. Two data sets are NOT transcribed and are generated in the
test file instead, which the file says: the change detection series and the
two-country quiz. Three more are used in part, also stated at their use.

